--
N5008 [expr.const]/4: a constexpr variable is usable in constant expressions
after its initializing declaration, whatever its storage duration;
[dcl.constexpr]/6: its initializer is a constant expression (manifestly
constant-evaluated), and constexpr declares the object const.  Three gaps
in the front end's constant evaluation: (1) the initializer of a constexpr
ARRAY (kept a real object, not a macro) was not type-checked in a constant-
expression context, so `classify(3)' stayed a call; (2) the constexpr
interpreter read array values only of objects with static storage duration,
so a constexpr LOCAL array passed by reference had no value; (3) a
modification of an element of a local array whose lifetime began within
the evaluation (`++seen[t]', [expr.const]/5 as relaxed in C++14) was not
supported.  This is libc++'s __get_comp_type / __compute_comp_type behind
common_comparison_category_t and std::compare_three_way (KNOWNBUG
cpp20_compare_three_way_pointers_libcxx).  Fixed in round 163b.
